†Research】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Core】【2†Protocol】【3†Proofs】【4†Economy】【5†RWA】【6†EIP】【7†Updates】\nL3: \nL4: Search research`Ctrl K`\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Core】【2†Protocol】【3†Proofs】【4†Economy】【5†RWA】【6†EIP】【7†Updates】\nL7: \nL8: 【0†← All research】 【1†CORE】\nL9: \nL10: Published\nL11:     20 Oct 2026\nL12: \nL13: Reading time\nL14:     20 min\nL15: \nL16: 0 0\nL17: \nL18: Cite this articleText or BibTeX Copy for AIMarkdown, with source\nL19: \nL20: Core\nL21: # What State Synchronization Asked of a GKR Prover\nL22: \nL23: 20 Oct 2026 · 20 min read\nL24: \nL25: In December 2025 we argued that GKR is a precondition for a state oracle to exist. Building the StateSync-GKR prover showed what an engine needs on top of it: three-state leaves, sequential keys, a fixed 118-layer schedule, sparse sumcheck booking, derived wiring, reused preparation and machine-checked acceptance. Measured end to end, delay moved outside the prover.\nL26: 【8†GitHub†github.com】 【9†Product site†oraclizer.io】 【10†SS-GKR docs†oraclizer.io】 【11†arXiv: formal verification†arxiv.org】 【12†arXiv: design and evaluation†arxiv.org】\nL27: \nL28: 【13†Image: StateSync-GKR prover: a few lit cells in a dark glass grid send fibers through rings to one point】\nL29: ## TL;DR\nL30:   * In December 2025 we argued that 【14†GKR†docs.oraclizer.io】 is not an optimization for a state oracle but a precondition for one to exist. Turning that claim into a running prover showed that a precondition does not make an engine. What else the engine needed was taught one item at a time, by the workload and by failures.\nL31:   * 【15†State synchronization†docs.oraclizer.io】 asks a prover narrow questions: is a record under a published root, is it absent, did a leaf change correctly. It asks them again and again, with circuits of the same shape, under a deadline. Three-state leaves, sequential keys, a 118-layer circuit that does not change with tree depth, one preparation and independent per-request proofs all follow from those questions.\nL32:   * Most of the January plan changed. The memory wall of a dense oracle, a verification cost hidden in the wiring table, a leaf-encoding defect masked by a placeholder hash, and vacuous theorems behind a clean build each changed the engine.\nL33:   * The published measurements show 89.46x lower peak resident memory than our own dense reference, verification 10.80 to 23.68x faster than table wiring, and a 3.46 to 4.45x shorter proving path from reused preparation. Measured all the way to the caller, the delay piled up in the queue outside proof computation, behind a serial path that returns results.\nL34:   * We still hold the original claim, and its grounds are now an engine we actually built, measured and machine-checked.\nL35: ## 1. Why We Built the StateSync-GKR Prover Ourselves\nL36: In December 2025, 【16†Why State Oracle Demand a Different Proof Primitive】 argued that data oracles and state oracles face different proof demands. A data oracle asks whether a value was correct at one moment. A state oracle has to keep asking whether every causally linked state transition is valid.\nL37: That article held that committing to every intermediate value made continuous synchronization possible in theory but infeasible in computation for existing SNARKs, and it set the GKR protocol, which checks each layer with 【17†sumcheck†docs.oraclizer.io】 and removes intermediate commitments, as the answer [1]. A month later, 【18†GKR Protocol Deep Dive: The Computational Engine Behind OSS】 laid out a plan to build that engine on gnark, with performance estimated by simulation.\nL38: Ten months after the December article, the engine exists. The Rust source of the 【19†StateSync-GKR†docs.oraclizer.io】 prover is public, and two arXiv papers carry its trust chain and its controlled measurements [5,6]. What we built barely resembles the January plan. We wrote Rust in place of gnark, and in place of another team's GKR library we implemented sumcheck and GKR to their published specifications on open-source field and hash primitives.\nL132:   * Compared against SNARK and STARK baselines on the same workload, and as approaches such as folding mature, where does the boundary of the precondition move?\nL133:   * How does throughput per unit of primary finality, including consensus and state commitment, relate to the inner proof throughput we measured?\nL134: ## References\nL135:   * [1] Goldwasser, S., Kalai, Y. T., & Rothblum, G. N. (2015). Delegating Computation: Interactive Proofs for Muggles. Journal of the ACM, 62(4). 【26†https://doi.org/10.1145/2699436†doi.org】\nL136:   * [2] Xie, T., Zhang, J., Zhang, Y., Papamanthou, C., & Song, D. (2019). Libra: Succinct Zero-Knowledge Proofs with Optimal Prover Computation. CRYPTO 2019. 【27†https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-26954-8_24†doi.org】\nL137:   * [3] Thaler, J. (2013). Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation. CRYPTO 2013. 【28†https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-40084-1_5†doi.org】\nL138:   * [4] Garvía Bosshard, A., Bootle, J., & Sprenger, C. (2024). Formal Verification of the Sumcheck Protocol. IEEE CSF 2024. 【29†https://doi.org/10.1109/CSF61375.2024.00014†doi.org】\nL139:   * [5] Kim, J. (2026). StateSync-GKR: Machine-Checking the Trust Chain from Sparse-Merkle State Transitions to GKR Verification. arXiv:2610.05335. 【11†https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05335†arxiv.org】\nL140:   * [6] Kim, J. (2026). StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine. arXiv:2610.14849. 【12†https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849†arxiv.org】\nL141:   * [7] Grassi, L., Khovratovich, D., & Schofnegger, M. (2023). Poseidon2: A Faster Version of the Poseidon Hash Function. AFRICACRYPT 2023. 【30†https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-37679-5_8†doi.org】\nL142:   * [8] Plonky3 contributors. (2026). Plonky3 v0.4.3. 【31†https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/releases/tag/v0.4.3†github.com】\nL143: 【32†PreviousSync Degree Enforcement: What Turns a Classification Into a Constraint】 【1†CORE More in Core26 articles, newest first Bro